1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,387
The best value gaming graphics card this Black Friday isn't made by Nvidia or AMD.

2
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:11,951
And one of the cards you'll see in a lot of deal banners this week belongs in the skip tier.

3
00:00:12,231 --> 00:00:23,019
We're going to rank every budget graphics card you can actually buy at the end of November 2026, using one number: how many dollars you pay for each frame.

4
00:00:23,299 --> 00:00:28,447
By the end, you'll know which card to buy, and the exact price where you should walk away.

5
00:00:29,197 --> 00:00:31,093
First, why this year is strange.

6
00:00:31,373 --> 00:00:36,825
Every card in this video sells for more today than it did at launch, some for more than double.

7
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:46,277
AI data centers are buying memory as fast as it can be made, and chipmakers have shifted production toward the memory those servers use.

8
00:00:46,557 --> 00:00:49,718
That leaves less of the memory gaming cards need.

9
00:00:49,998 --> 00:01:02,171
Nvidia has reportedly raised prices to its board partners several times this year, and AMD reportedly told partners to expect about ten percent more for the last quarter.

10
00:01:02,451 --> 00:01:04,228
So launch prices are history.

11
00:01:04,508 --> 00:01:08,893
We'll use what US stores were actually listing in the middle of November.

12
00:01:09,643 --> 00:01:11,262
Here's how the ranking works.

13
00:01:11,542 --> 00:01:18,863
Every card gets a speed score at 1080p, where the RTX 50 60 counts as 100.

14
00:01:19,143 --> 00:01:26,974
The scores come from averaging several independent reviews, and they're rounded, so treat a gap of a few points as a tie.

15
00:01:27,254 --> 00:01:30,024
Then we divide each card's price by its score.

16
00:01:30,304 --> 00:01:33,221
That gives dollars per point, and lower is better.

17
00:01:33,501 --> 00:01:40,957
Prices change every day, so for every card you'll also get a walk-away price, the point where it stops being worth it.

18
00:01:41,707 --> 00:01:43,393
The lineup has six cards.

19
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:51,841
Intel's Arc B570 with 10 gigabytes of video memory, and the Arc B580 with 12.

20
00:01:52,121 --> 00:01:58,115
Nvidia's RTX 50 50 and RTX 50 60, both with 8 gigabytes.

21
00:01:58,395 --> 00:02:05,024
AMD's Radeon RX 90 60 XT, sold with either 8 or 16 gigabytes.

22
00:02:05,304 --> 00:02:11,721
And one more that keeps sneaking into Black Friday deals, the RTX 30 50 with 6 gigabytes.

23
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:13,366
Let's start at the bottom.

24
00:02:14,116 --> 00:02:18,276
Skip tier, card one: the RTX 30 50 6 gigabyte.

25
00:02:18,556 --> 00:02:37,789
It carries the same name as the 8 gigabyte RTX 30 50, but it has fewer cores, 2,304 instead of 2,560, on a narrower memory bus, so it moves 168 gigabytes per second instead of 224.

26
00:02:38,069 --> 00:02:49,745
It's built to draw just 70 watts, and early benchmarks put it about twenty percent behind the 8 gigabyte version, which was already the entry-level card of its generation.

27
00:02:50,025 --> 00:02:56,235
If a cheap prebuilt lists an RTX 30 50 with 6 gigabytes, that's where the money was saved.

28
00:02:56,985 --> 00:03:02,209
Skip tier, card two, and this one surprises people: the RTX 50 50.

29
00:03:02,489 --> 00:03:03,823
It isn't a bad chip.

30
00:03:04,103 --> 00:03:17,585
It scores about 78, roughly the same as Intel's cheaper B570, and TechSpot measured the Arc B580 about fourteen percent faster across sixteen games.

31
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:19,504
The problem is the price.

32
00:03:19,784 --> 00:03:26,039
It launched at 249 dollars, and in mid November Best Buy listed it at 400.

33
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:31,951
That's about 5 dollars and 13 cents per point, with only 8 gigabytes of memory.

34
00:03:32,231 --> 00:03:42,167
Walk-away price: anything above what the B570 costs, because the B570 matches it and gives you 2 more gigabytes.

35
00:03:42,917 --> 00:03:48,942
B tier: the RTX 50 60 and the 8 gigabyte RX 90 60 XT.

36
00:03:49,222 --> 00:03:51,970
Together they set our yardstick of 100.

37
00:03:52,250 --> 00:04:09,256
In TechPowerUp's testing the 16 gigabyte Radeon was about three and a half percent faster than the RTX 50 60 at 1080p, and the 8 gigabyte version about five percent slower than the 16, so these two are a tie.

38
00:04:09,536 --> 00:04:18,307
The RTX 50 60 launched at 299 dollars and was listed around 500 on Amazon in mid November.

39
00:04:18,587 --> 00:04:20,926
That's exactly 5 dollars per point.

40
00:04:21,206 --> 00:04:31,486
Both have 8 gigabytes, which still works at 1080p, but costs you real frames at 1440p with ultra textures.

41
00:04:31,766 --> 00:04:38,509
Verdict: if you want one of these, buy whichever is cheaper on the day, and don't pay more than 400.

42
00:04:39,259 --> 00:04:45,590
Now the most interesting card in the list: the RX 90 60 XT with 16 gigabytes.

43
00:04:45,870 --> 00:04:53,731
It's the fastest card here, at about 105, and it's the cheapest way to get 16 gigabytes of video memory.

44
00:04:54,011 --> 00:04:56,704
It launched at 349 dollars.

45
00:04:56,984 --> 00:05:00,797
In mid November, Newegg listed one at 750.

46
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:07,053
That's over 7 dollars per point, the worst value on this list, for the best card on this list.

47
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:09,183
Great card, terrible price.

48
00:05:09,463 --> 00:05:12,754
Its walk-away price is 420 dollars.

49
00:05:13,034 --> 00:05:19,238
At or below that, it's the one I'd buy, because 16 gigabytes keeps it useful for years.

50
00:05:19,988 --> 00:05:22,733
A tier: Intel's Arc B580.

51
00:05:23,013 --> 00:05:30,679
It scores about 88, so it's a bit slower than the RTX 50 60, but it has 12 gigabytes of memory.

52
00:05:30,959 --> 00:05:38,771
It launched at 249 dollars, and in November it's been averaging around 390 at US stores.

53
00:05:39,051 --> 00:05:42,466
That's about 4 dollars and 43 cents per point.

54
00:05:42,746 --> 00:05:50,279
Cheaper per frame than every Nvidia and AMD card on this list, with more memory than any 8 gigabyte card.

55
00:05:51,029 --> 00:05:56,772
And S tier, the best value this Black Friday: Intel's Arc B570.

56
00:05:57,052 --> 00:06:09,053
It scores about 78, the same as the RTX 50 50, and in reviews it landed within about seven percent of the RTX 40 60 at 1080p.

57
00:06:09,333 --> 00:06:11,261
It has 10 gigabytes of memory.

58
00:06:11,541 --> 00:06:18,217
It launched at 219 dollars, and on November 18th Newegg listed it at 289.

59
00:06:18,497 --> 00:06:24,112
That works out to about 3 dollars and 71 cents per point, the lowest on the board.

60
00:06:24,392 --> 00:06:30,288
It costs 111 dollars less than the RTX 50 50 for the same speed.

61
00:06:31,038 --> 00:06:35,021
But there's a catch, and it decides whether Intel is right for you.

62
00:06:35,301 --> 00:06:43,838
Arc cards need a feature called Resizable BAR switched on in your motherboard's settings, or they lose a lot of performance.

63
00:06:44,118 --> 00:06:51,219
And on older processors, Intel's driver has needed more CPU time than AMD's or Nvidia's.

64
00:06:51,499 --> 00:07:00,875
When TechSpot paired the B580 with a Ryzen 5 2600, it ran about twenty percent slower than it should have.

65
00:07:01,155 --> 00:07:05,089
Newer drivers improved this, but some games are still affected.

66
00:07:05,369 --> 00:07:17,851
So if your PC is several years old, or your board doesn't offer Resizable BAR, skip Arc and pick the RX 90 60 XT or the RTX 50 60 instead.

67
00:07:18,601 --> 00:07:21,986
Here's how to judge any Black Friday deal in ten seconds.

68
00:07:22,266 --> 00:07:24,698
Aim for 4 dollars per point or less.

69
00:07:24,978 --> 00:07:30,891
That means the B570 is a good deal under about 310 dollars.

70
00:07:31,171 --> 00:07:34,468
The B580 under 350.

71
00:07:34,748 --> 00:07:39,868
The RTX 50 60 or the 8 gigabyte Radeon under 400.

72
00:07:40,148 --> 00:07:44,041
And the 16 gigabyte Radeon under 420.

73
00:07:44,321 --> 00:07:50,917
If a card misses its number, it isn't a deal, no matter what the banner says the original price was.

74
00:07:51,667 --> 00:07:53,049
So here's the verdict.

75
00:07:53,329 --> 00:08:01,424
If your PC is from the last few years and you play at 1080p, buy the Arc B570.

76
00:08:01,704 --> 00:08:07,005
Step up to the B580 only if you find it under 350.

77
00:08:07,285 --> 00:08:13,652
Skip the RTX 50 50 and the 6 gigabyte RTX 30 50 at today's prices.

78
00:08:13,932 --> 00:08:21,807
And if a 16 gigabyte RX 90 60 XT ever drops near 420 dollars, buy it on the spot.

79
00:08:22,087 --> 00:08:24,402
It's the card that will last the longest.

80
00:08:25,152 --> 00:08:34,091
Next Saturday: DDR4 or DDR5 in 2026, and why this year's memory prices changed the answer.

81
00:08:34,371 --> 00:08:39,062
There's a new verdict every Saturday, so subscribe and you'll catch the next one.

